    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d396">

                <pb facs="0515" n="430" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg430"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3128: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Acting Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-06-06T12:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 6, 1919,
                                noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 7, 7.38
                            a.m.</hi>]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Your June 3, 3 p.m. Situation unchanged since last telegram May 31, 1 p.m.<anchor
                        n="38" xml:id="d396-f1"/> Japanese Minister has never proposed any action to
                    me although when I asked him whether anything was being done he also laid great
                    emphasis on the irrelevant contention that American soldiers had been in the
                    Japanese concession. See my telegram of May 3 [<hi rendition="#italic">13</hi>],
                    4 p.m.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Reinsch</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d396-f1" n="38" xml:id="d396-f1-content">
                <p>Probably refers to his undated telegram, <ref target="#pg429">p. 429</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
